<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Turkish — AM11a.

  Storage stays canonical English (SPEC-ANDROID hard rule 7). Every string here
  is a LABEL; not one of them is ever written to a watch.toml. The enum* values
  are the clearest case: `enum_kind_automatic` reads "Otomatik" on screen while
  "Automatic" is what lands in the file and what the desktop reads back.

  The vocabulary is the desktop's. Every term that saat_tr.ts already
  translates is reused verbatim rather than translated again — 228 of these
  strings came from there — so a collector reading the two apps sees one
  vocabulary and not two opinions about what a "lug" is called.

  Plurals carry `one` AND `other`, with the SAME WORDING in both. Turkish does
  have a `one` category in CLDR (i = 1 and v = 0) — an earlier draft of this
  file assumed it did not and Android lint said otherwise, which is the sort of
  thing to check rather than to reason about. The two forms read alike because
  the grammar, not the category, is what differs from English: a Turkish noun
  does not agree with a numeral, so "1 gün" and "3 gün" are both correct and
  neither takes a plural suffix.

  StringsTranslationTest asserts this file covers every key in values/, so a
  string added later cannot quietly ship untranslated.
-->
